{
  "title": "Infantile Blount's Disease (tibia vara)",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pediatrics/4050/infantile-blounts-disease-tibia-vara",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Feb 10 2026",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Infantile Blount's disease is progressive pathologic genu varum centered at the tibia in children 2 to 5 years of age.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is suspected clinically with presence of a genu varum/flexion/internal rotation deformity and confirmed radiographically with an increased metaphyseal-diaphyseal angle.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment ranges from bracing to surgery depending on patient age, severity of deformity, and presence of a physeal bar.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "4",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Risk factors",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "2",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "overweight children",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "early walkers (< 1 year)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "Hispanic and African American",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Best divided into two distinct disease entities",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**Infantile Blount's**(this topic)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "pathologic genu varum in children 2 to 5 years of age",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "male > female",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "more common",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "bilateral in 50%",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "**Adolescent Blount's**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "pathologic genu varum in children > 10 years of age",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "less common",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "less severe",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "more likely to be unilateral",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "likely multifactorial but related to **mechanical overload** in **genetically susceptible** individuals including",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "excessive medial pressure produces an osteochondrosis of the medial proximal tibial physis and epiphysis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "osteochondrosis can progress to a physeal bar",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "2",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Genu varum is a normal physiologic process in children",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**physiologic genu varum**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "genu varum (**bowed legs**) is normal in children **less than 2 years**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "genu varum migrates to a **neutral at ~ 14 months**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "continues on to a **peak genu valgum (knocked knees) at ~ 3 years** of age",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "genu valgum then migrates back to normal physiologic valgus at ~ 7 years of age",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Classification",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Langenskiold Classification",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/4050/images/langen.jpg",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "importance": "2",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "type I thru IV consist of increasing medial metaphyseal beaking and sloping",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "type V and VI have an epiphyseal-metaphyseal bony bridge (congenital bar across physis)",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "provides prognostic guidelines",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "2",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "",
          "table": [
            {
              "header": true,
              "cells": [
                {
